# Hooks

A custom hook shares stateful logic. Each call has its own state. To share the state itself, lift it into context.

The name starts with `use` and a capital letter. A function that calls no hook is a plain function, such as `mapProductCard`.

One hook per file. `useProductCards` lives in `use-product-cards.ts`. The name is the use case: what it takes and what it returns.

Extract a hook when the same stateful logic is copied, or when an Effect synchronizes with something outside React. The component then states the intent. Do not wrap a single `useState`. Do not add a lifecycle wrapper such as `useMount`, `useEffectOnce`, or `useUpdateEffect`.

The body runs on every render, so it stays pure. Call it unconditionally, and pass reactive values in as arguments. An event handler that an Effect calls is wrapped in `useEffectEvent`, so a new function does not re-subscribe the Effect.

Mapping stays in a mapper from `@/mappers`. The hook calls that mapper. Wrap the call in `useMemo` only when the memoization rule says so. UI state stays in the component. The feature calls the hook and does not map the query itself.

```tsx
// Bad: the screen maps the query, and a sort is named like a hook
const cards = (products.data ?? []).map(mapProductCard);

function useSorted(items: readonly Product[]) {
  return items.toSorted();
}

// Good
export function useProductCards() {
  const products = useProducts();

  const cards = (products.data ?? []).map(mapProductCard);

  return { cards, isLoading: products.isLoading, error: products.error };
}
```
